# A value the compiler may not work out.
#
# Everything in the other examples is known before the program runs: `add`
# is recursion over constructors and its arguments are constructors, so the
# tier above works the answer out and what a target sees is the number. That
# is what you want of a program and exactly not what you want of a test of
# one, since a test that folds away tests nothing.
#
# `opaque!` answers what it was given. Nothing else about it is so: no
# target asks anything of the outside for it, and what it costs is a move.
# It is an effect because the working-out is what must stop at it, and a
# name with no body is the one thing that tier cannot read through.

type Nat Zero Succ(Nat)

add(a Zero) -> a
add(a Succ(k)) -> Succ(add(a k))

main!(h) ->
  two = opaque(Succ(Succ(Zero)))
  exit!(h add(two two))
